#!/bin/sh
# Refuse a commit message that would close the reference it only meant to name.
#
# Why this exists. On 2026-10-16 a documentation commit on `main` carried the
# line "both are merged and resolved: #18, where both sides appended an item".
# It was prose about a pull request. GitHub read `resolved: #18` as an
# instruction, closed pull request #18 -- four answered review rounds -- and
# attributed the act to whoever pushed, so the timeline accused a person while
# the cause sat in a commit message nobody had reason to re-read. It took three
# wrong readings to find, and the `closed` event had carried the commit id all
# along.
#
# What GitHub actually does: `close`, `fix` and `resolve`, in every form, with
# an optional colon, applied to the reference that follows -- `#12`, `GH-12`,
# `owner/repo#12`, or a full issue or pull URL -- and it applies them to pull
# requests as well as issues. Only commit messages on the default branch and
# pull request descriptions trigger it; ordinary comments do not.
#
# A commit may of course mean to close an issue. That is what the override is
# for: a guard that forbids a real workflow gets worked around rather than used.
set -u

msg_file="$1"

# **Comment lines are read, and that is a correction.** This dropped every line
# beginning with `#`, on the premise that git strips them. Git strips them only
# under `--cleanup=strip`, which is the default **when the message comes from an
# editor**. With `-m` or `-F` -- which is how an agent commits -- the default is
# `whitespace` and those lines are stored verbatim. Measured 2026-10-20:
# `git commit -m 'docs: x' -m '#18 is merged, and this closes #20'` committed
# with rc 0 and the line intact. A 72-column paragraph wraps onto a `#NN` often
# enough for that to matter, and `core.commentChar` can be something else again.
#
# So the hook reads what might be stored. Editor mode still passes, because
# git's template carries no keyword in front of a reference, and the
# `--verbose` diff sits below the scissors, which is still cut. Verified across
# all 510 non-merge messages on `main`: the verdicts are unchanged, and the one
# refusal is still 5aa5bc9.
#
# The line numbers now match the file, which they did not while lines were
# being dropped.
body=$(sed -e '/^------------------------ >8 /,$d' "$msg_file")

# `(^|[^[:alnum:]_])` because the alternation had no left anchor, so any word
# **ending** in one of these counted: `prefix #12`, `hotfix #12`, `bugfix #12`,
# `the unresolved #12 thread`, `disclosed: #12`, `enclosed #12` were all
# refused. GitHub's keywords are words, and "unresolved" beside a reference is
# ordinary prose in this repository's own reviews.
keywords='(^|[^[:alnum:]_])(close|closes|closed|fix|fixes|fixed|resolve|resolves|resolved)'
ref='(#|[Gg][Hh]-|[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+#)[0-9]+'
url='https?://github\.com/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/(issues|pull)/[0-9]+'
pattern="${keywords}[[:space:]]*:?[[:space:]]*(${ref}|${url})"

hits=$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | grep -niE "$pattern" || true)

# **A second pass over adjacent pairs**, used only when the first finds nothing.
# `grep` reads one line at a time, so the incident's own sentence wrapped two
# words earlier -- `resolved:` ending a line and `#18, where both sides` opening
# the next -- went straight through. Whether GitHub closes across a line break
# is **not verified**: measuring it needs a commit pushed to a default branch.
# The costs are lopsided, which is what decides it. A false refusal costs a
# rephrase; a false pass costs a closed pull request with its review rounds.
#
# The reported line number is the **first** of the pair, which is where the
# keyword is and so where the edit goes.
if [ -z "$hits" ]; then
  hits=$(printf '%s\n' "$body" \
    | awk 'NR > 1 { print NR - 1 ":" prev " " $0 } { prev = $0 }' \
    | grep -iE "$pattern" || true)
fi

# Is this copy still the one `main` carries? Computed, not assumed -- the whole
# point of installing a copy into the shared hooks directory is that no checkout
# can switch it off, and the price of a copy is that it can fall behind. The
# installer writes the source blob id beside it; a missing file, a missing
# `origin/main` or an agent clone that has neither simply says nothing.
guard_dir=$(dirname "$0")
if [ -f "${guard_dir}/commit-msg.blob" ]; then
  installed=$(cat "${guard_dir}/commit-msg.blob" 2>/dev/null || true)
  current=$(git rev-parse --verify -q 'origin/main:scripts/hooks/commit-msg' 2>/dev/null || true)
  if [ -n "$installed" ] && [ -n "$current" ] && [ "$installed" != "$current" ]; then
    echo "commit-msg: this guard is older than main's -- re-run scripts/hooks/install.sh" >&2
  fi
fi

[ -n "$hits" ] || exit 0

if [ "${ALLOW_CLOSING_KEYWORD:-0}" = "1" ]; then
  printf 'commit-msg: closing keyword allowed by ALLOW_CLOSING_KEYWORD=1:\n%s\n' "$hits" >&2
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<MSG

commit-msg refused this message: it would close the reference it names.

$hits

GitHub reads close, fix and resolve -- in any form, with or without a colon --
as an instruction to close whatever reference follows, on pull requests as well
as issues, and it names whoever pushes as the actor rather than the commit. A
sentence that merely talks about a pull request closes it.

To describe one, put a word between the verb and the number: "the #18 conflict",
"on #18", "answered on #18". To close an issue on purpose:

    ALLOW_CLOSING_KEYWORD=1 git commit ...

The message is kept in ${msg_file}; nothing was committed.
MSG
exit 1
